> Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:9:- The VR seat is an inspector, not a superintendent (revised October 14, evening). It sees the 
true state of the site, reports it, holds a stop-work authority, and confirms whether the office's orders reached the 
crews. It no longer moves machines, places protection or enters codes; Plan does those as plan changes.
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:10:- The seats split as BOMBANANA splits its monkeys: Site sees but cannot act, Rules knows but 
cannot act, Plan acts but cannot see. On the chart, Plan owns the planned lines, Rules owns the limits (permit 
boundaries and hold points), Site owns the actual lines.
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:11:- The monitor's actual line is a day old, from the daily report; Site is live. Orders take time 
to reach the field, and field deviations (a crew that keeps working, a box at the wrong station) are events only Site 
can catch.
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:12:- Events slow the world instead of freezing it, so talk costs days. Four zones, the identical 
seed in Stage 2, rain rework that scales with open trench, and demobilization are decided or recommended below.
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:13:- Movement in VR is a site truck on the haul road with a stop at every station and a dismount 
to inspect. No free locomotion.
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:14:- The field manual follows the Keep Talking pattern and adds procedures that Rules reads aloud, 
Captain Sonar style.
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:15:- Stage 1 (push) has no huddle and the PM refuses preparation; Stage 2 (pull) adds a 30 second 
huddle and permits; Stage 3 is Florida, Las Vegas or Los Angeles. A conflict in Stage 1 stops the job and plays as an 
argument in VR.
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:16:- Workbook values are validated by Phumpat and Dr. Rybkowski; the list for them is under 
Numbers.
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:17:
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:18:## The team and the three levers
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:19:
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:20:A Line of Balance shows planned lines, actual lines, and the limits between them. Each seat 
owns one of the three, and the agency splits the way BOMBANANA splits its three monkeys. This is the design's core 
statement: learning the chart and playing the game are the same activity, seen from three sides.
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:21:
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:22:| Seat | Platform | Owns on the LOB | Agency | In construction terms |
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:23:| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:24:| Site | Quest; the 2D Site page for players without a headset | The actual lines: what the 
crews really did today, seen live | Sees but cannot act: inspect, flag, stop work, confirm | Field inspector, the 
owner's representative |
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:25:| Plan | Monitor (web page) | The planned lines: start days, machine assignments and orders, 
holds and resumes, and the money | Acts but cannot see: every change to the plan and every payment | Scheduler and the 
PM's office |
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:26:| Rules | Paper | The limits: permit boundaries (open trench) and hold points | Knows but 
cannot act: diagnose, prescribe, issue permits, sign inspections | The spec book and the permit desk |
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:27:
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:28:Everyone reads the LOB, from different sides. The monitor shows planned lines against reported 
actuals, which arrive at the end of each day, with uncertainty bands and money. The site trailer board in VR shows the 
live actual lines and the permit limits, never the plan. The paper ledger records permit, pipe, inspection and 
backfill days per zone. Site is the only seat that sees today; Plan is the only seat that can change tomorrow.
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:29:
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:30:|  | Site only | Plan only | Rules only |
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:31:| --- | --- | --- | --- |
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:32:| Sees | The site live: trench walls and water, the sky, machine plates (Small, Standard, 
Heavy), pipe stencils and damage, protection in place or missing, crews working, idle or arguing, trucks at the gate, 
the permit board at each zone | Planned lines, reported actuals as of yesterday, uncertainty bands, budget, deadline, 
inbox (supplier dates, forecast percentages, inspector calendar, PM mail), catalog prices and lead days, the radio log 
of orders sent | Field manual (situation rules, response effects, required checks, procedures), city page, permit book 
with codes, hold-point checklists, open-trench limit |
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:33:| Lacks | The plan, the rules, the money, the forecast | The ground, the rules, whether an 
order was carried out | The ground, the plan, the money |
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:34:
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:35:Two rules follow. No event can be diagnosed, decided or executed from one seat. No order is 
known to have worked until Site confirms it.
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:36:
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:37:## What each player can do
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:38:
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:39:Site's verbs are an inspector's verbs: look, report, stop, confirm. Everything that changes the 
plan belongs to Plan, and everything that releases work belongs to Rules. Site never moves a machine, places 
protection, holds a crew beyond a safety stop, or enters a code.
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:40:
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:41:**Site (VR)**
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:42:
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:43:| Verb | How | What it changes |
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:44:| --- | --- | --- |
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:45:| Ride and dismount | The site truck runs along the haul road and stops at any station stake; 
dismount to stand at the stake. No walking beyond arm's reach | Where Site is, which decides what it can see and which 
crew an order reaches at once |
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:46:| Inspect | Look at a trench wall, a pipe, a machine plate, a delivery ticket or the sky for 
two seconds; the clipboard reads the indicator out | Turns a range on the monitor into a fact; reveals an event's 
variant (the game hides E1 and P3 variants until seen) |
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:47:| Flag | Plant a flag at a station | An unlabeled marker on the monitor's LOB and in the 
ledger; Plan has to ask what it is |
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:48:| Stop work | Raise the red flag at a station; the crews in that zone halt within the hour and 
keep billing | A safety hold that stands until Plan commits a plan or resumes; the inspector's one authority |
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:49:| Confirm | After an order, watch the crew and press confirm, or report that the order was not 
carried out | Closes the loop; an unconfirmed order is the commonest field deviation |
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:50:| Listen | Stand with the foremen at a conflict or an idle crew; their lines give the cause | 
The only account of why, which Plan needs to choose between holding and demobilizing |
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:51:
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:52:**Plan (monitor)**
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:53:
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:54:| Verb | How | What it changes |
> Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:98:## The VR seat
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:99:
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:100:Site is an inspector. The seat is built around noticing, with one authority, because free 
movement and a long verb list are what make VR tiring and what unbalanced the first draft. Site never changes the 
plan: moving a machine, placing protection, holding a crew beyond a safety stop and entering codes are Plan's.
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:101:
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:102:- **Movement.** A site truck runs on the haul road beside the trench and stops at any station 
stake; the player dismounts to stand at the stake. The cab is a fixed frame of reference, turns are snap turns, and 
there is no free locomotion. Riding is not idle time: an order reaches a crew at once only when Site is standing 
there, so Site chooses where to be.
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:103:- **What only Site sees.** The site as it is today: trench walls and water, protection in 
place or missing, machine plates, pipe stencils and damage, delivery tickets, the sky, trucks queued at the gate, the 
permit number posted at each zone board, and crews working, idle or arguing. Held crews get visibly angrier the longer 
they stand, and the daily report never says so. The monitor's actual line is yesterday's feet; Site is live.
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:104:- **The clipboard.** Three buttons on the wrist: Flag, Stop work, Confirm. Inspection needs no 
button; looking at an indicator for two seconds reads it out. Controllers first; the four verbs map to hand tracking 
on the Quest 3S later without redesign.
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:105:- **Field deviations.** A new event class, one or two per stage, that only Site can catch:
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:106:  1. F1, order not received: a crew keeps working after a hold, or keeps digging after a stop. 
An order lands at the next shift unless Site is there.
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:107:  2. F2, pushing ahead: excavation crosses a zone boundary without a permit, and the city 
fines it. Natural in Stage 1, where every permit was issued; an authored deviation in Stage 2.
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:108:  3. F3, wrong station: protection or a lent machine arrives at the wrong stake.
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:109:  4. F4, wrong delivery: the pipe stencil does not match the purchase order (a P2 variant).
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:110:  5. F5, late arrival: a lent or ordered machine has not shown up when the plan says it has 
(the remobilization delay).
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:111:- **The argument.** At a conflict the foremen meet at the station and say why: the schedule 
put them a day apart, and a crane lays 180 ft a day while a loader backfills 250. Site listens and reports; Plan 
chooses between holding the crew and demobilizing it; Site confirms whichever happened.
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:112:
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:113:## The field manual
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:114:
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:115:The manual is the workbook rewritten the way the Keep Talking manual is written. Site 
describes what it sees, the manual branches on that description plus the city, and nothing in it names the choice. Ben 
Kane's rule applies: an expert role that is only dense reading does not make good play, so every page ends in a 
question for another seat.
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:116:
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:117:1. **Front page, one rule.** Days lost to an event = rate drop × duration. The crew behind 
idles for whatever exceeds its buffer. Checked in simulation: late pipe (P2) at 50% for 3 days costs 1.5 days; a 1 day 
buffer idles backfill half a day, 1.5 days idles nothing.
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:118:2. **City pages**, one per city: the Hazard Matrix row, the level signature, which situations 
can occur (Texas has no rock, so no E2; Las Vegas has no water or rain), and how responses change here (congested 
cities add 1 to 2 lead days and forbid sloping the trench; running sand halves a trench box's benefit and calls for 
shoring). The city's open-trench limit.
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:119:3. **Indicator tables** ("What am I looking at?"), keyed by Site's words and the city rating. 
Water seeping and a wall that slumps when touched, with groundwater High or V.High: treat as Type C, collapse likely 
(P1), box or shoring, no sloping where the city is congested. Machine stopped, operator out of the cab, orange flag on 
a line: E1. Pipe on the ground beside the truck, boom down: P3; inspect the joint, cracked is damage, whole is a 
safety hold only. Forecast 70% on the monitor and towers to the south in VR: A2 within a day.
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:120:4. **Situation pages** (E1, E2, P1, P2, P3, B1, A1, A2): cause, what it hits, rate drop, 
duration, hidden consequence, then options A to D with their effect on rate, other crews, cost and days, and the check 
each needs with the seat that verifies it. The workbook's When Right and When Wrong verdicts are not printed (R5); the 
checks they imply are.
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:121:5. **Permit and hold-point rules**: the open-trench limit, the checklist before backfill 
(tracer wire placed, bedding placed, compaction test passed), and what Site must confirm before Rules signs.
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:122:6. **Lookup tables**: days lost by drop and duration, machine rates, lead days by city, and 
the costs of holding or demobilizing a crew.
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:123:7. **Procedures**: numbered checklists that Rules reads aloud, one line per seat, the way 
Captain Sonar's crew calls its turns. Stop work (Site raises the flag, Site reports, Rules names, Plan commits, Site 
confirms, Rules logs). Zone handshake. Lend a machine. Resume. Demobilize. A procedure read aloud is faster than a 
discussion, and the clock is running.
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:124:
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:125:Sample situation page, P1 trench collapse. Prices and multipliers are the game's own values 
(site\_catalog\_v2); durations are the workbook's.
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:126:
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:127:|  |  |
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:128:| --- | --- |
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:129:| Hits | Pipe laying, full stop |
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:130:| Cause | Sidewall failure from inadequate shoring or sloping |
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:131:| Duration | 3, 2 or 1 days to clear with a Small, Standard or Heavy excavator; the clearing 
excavator stops digging for those days |
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:132:| Hidden consequence | Clearing with your own machine moves the delay to excavation |
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:133:| A. Clear with your own excavator | Pipe at 0% until cleared, then 100%. Check: an excavator 
on site (Site reads the plate), and excavation's lead over pipe of at least the clearing days (Plan reads the LOB) |
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:134:| B. Emergency shoring | $18,000 plus $150 a day, work resumes in 1 day. Check: budget (Plan) |
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:135:| C. Hire an outside excavator | Catalog price, 3, 2 or 1 days by size, plus this city's lead 
days. Check: lead days (Rules' city page) and budget (Plan) |
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:136:| Before it happens | A trench box ($14,000 plus $90 a day) cuts likelihood to 0.35 and 
severity to 0.5; shoring ($32,000 plus $180 a day) to 0.2 and 0.4; sloping ($6,000) to 0.45 and 0.7, not allowed in 
New York, Los Angeles or Bangkok |
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:137:
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:138:## Paper kit
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:139:
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:140:Everything on paper is generated from the stage's scenario file, so the codes, stations and 
city match the build. One stage fits in an envelope.
  Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:141:
